{{Tocright}}'''Haste''' is a category of [[champion statistic|stats]] that reduce the '''total''' cooldown of non-[[basic attack]] [[Terminology#Spell|spells]], and thus improves their '''casting rate'''. It is the analog to [[attack speed]] for basic attacks. '''Haste''' does not affect static cooldowns nor target immunity cooldowns, unless otherwise stated. Haste is categorized based on the spell it affects; [[#Ability haste|champion ability haste]] ([[#Basic ability haste|basic]], [[#Ultimate haste|ultimate]]), [[#Item haste|item haste]], [[#Summoner spell haste|summoner spell haste]], and [[#Trinket haste|trinket haste]].

In [[Season 2021|Pre-Season 2021]], '''haste''' replaced {{stil|cooldown reduction}}, a statistic that multiplicatively increased casting rate and mandated an artificial cap and limited access from [[item]]s and [[rune]]s.<ref>Yetter, M. (7 August 2020). ''[https://na.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/news/riot-games/preseason-2021-champion-class-item-goals/ Preseason 2021 Class Item Goals]''. leagueoflegends.com.</ref> Every point of haste has the same benefit compared to the base casting rate; analogous to the way armor and magic resist are calculated.

== Reducing ability cooldowns ==
{{Main|Haste/Reducing ability cooldowns}}
'''Haste''' is a generic means of reducing cooldowns, but it is not the only method. Some effects' '''current''' (remaining) cooldown may decrease upon a condition being fulfilled.

== {{Anchor|Formula}}Haste formula ==
Assuming an effect is used as soon as it is available, and ignoring [[cast time]], its base casting rate is definitionally equal to&emsp;<math>\pagecolor{Black}\color{White}\frac{\rm 1\ cast}{\rm Base\ Cooldown}</math>&emsp;(inverse cooldown).

Haste modifies the casting rate of the effect by a percentage, at a 1:1 ratio. This is expressed using the following formula:

<math>\pagecolor{Black}\color{White}{\rm New\ Casting\ Rate}={\rm Base\ Casting\ Rate} \times \big(1 + \frac{\rm Haste}{100}\big)</math>

The casting rate therefore increases (for positive values) or decreases (for negative values) linearly, by 1% for every 1 point of haste. 100 haste means a 100% increase to the original casting rate, or double. 200 haste means a 200% increase to the original casting rate, or triple.

To find the resulting cooldown, combine the expression in brackets and substitute casting rate for inverse cooldown:

<math>\pagecolor{Black}\color{White}\frac{1}{\rm New\ Cooldown}=\frac{1}{\rm Base\ Cooldown} \times \frac{100 + {\rm Haste}}{100}</math>

Via invertendo property, the above is equivalent to:

<math>\pagecolor{Black}\color{White}{\rm New\ Cooldown}={\rm Base\ Cooldown}\times\frac{100}{100+{\rm Haste}}</math>

The cooldown therefore decreases (for positive values) or increases (for negative values) as haste increases via hyperbolic growth. 100 haste means the resulting cooldown is 50% of the original cooldown, or half. 200 haste means the resulting cooldown is 33.{{recurring|3}}% of the original cooldown, or a third.

Cooldown reduction can be found by manipulation of the above formula.

<math>\pagecolor{Black}\color{White}\frac{\rm New\ Cooldown}{\rm Base\ Cooldown}=\frac{100}{100+{\rm Haste}}</math>&emsp;<span style="color:grey;font-size:87%;">(Take the fraction of new over the old cooldown to find the resulting cooldown percentage)</span><br><br>

<math>\pagecolor{Black}\color{White}1-\frac{\rm New\ Cooldown}{\rm Base\ Cooldown}=1-\frac{100}{100+{\rm Haste}}</math>&emsp;<span style="color:grey;font-size:87%;">(Take the additive inverse of both sides to find the cooldown reduction)</span><br><br>

<math>\pagecolor{Black}\color{White}{\rm Cooldown\ Reduction}=\frac{100+{\rm Haste}}{100+{\rm Haste}}-\frac{100}{100+{\rm Haste}}</math>&emsp;<span style="color:grey;font-size:87%;">(Combine fractions)</span><br><br>

<math>\pagecolor{Black}\color{White}{\rm Cooldown\ Reduction}=\frac{100+{\rm Haste}-100}{100+{\rm Haste}}</math><br><br>

<math>\pagecolor{Black}\color{White}{\rm Cooldown\ Reduction}=\frac{\rm Haste}{100+{\rm Haste}}</math>

The formula shows that cooldown reduction can never reach exactly 100%, for a real cooldown of 0, by adding more haste: each additional point of haste gives less cooldown reduction. However, '''haste''' itself does not have diminishing returns in regards to '''casting speed'''. Conversely, cooldown reduction as a percentage value has increasing, potentially unlimited returns that bring the '''casting speed''' to infinity as it approaches 100%. When considering other factors such as potential cast times, human imperfect input and other gameplay variables, cooldown reduction can be a difficult statistic to intuit and calculate the value of.

